# Active Builds

_Copy to `ACTIVE-BUILDS.md` (gitignored — this is yours, not the public
template's) once your target role or market is specific enough to name
actual projects instead of picking from the generic archetype menu in
`curriculum/07-smb-capstones.md`. When this file exists and has entries,
the agent should treat it as the primary work plan, ahead of the generic
archetypes — see `AGENTS.md`._

## Why override the generic menu
The archetypes in `curriculum/07-smb-capstones.md` are good defaults when
you don't yet know exactly who you're building for. Once you do — a
specific business model, a specific target employer type, a specific
market — a handful of named, real projects mapped tightly to that target
beats an open-ended menu. This file is where you record that plan so the
agent stops treating it as one option among six and starts treating it as
the curriculum.

## Format, per project

### [Project name]
- **What it is:** one or two sentences.
- **Why this one, for this goal:** how it maps to your actual target
  (a business model, a role type, a market).
- **Technical gaps to close:** be specific — not "learn RAG," but "grounding
  a narration layer in structured data without hallucinating unlisted
  numbers." Note explicitly where the source curriculum (`curriculum/00-overview.md`
  phase mapping) actually covers the gap vs. where it doesn't (some things —
  vendor API quirks, git hygiene, domain-specific edge cases — aren't AI
  curriculum content at all, and pretending otherwise wastes time looking
  for a lesson that isn't coming).
- **Sequencing rationale:** why this project is where it is in the order —
  revenue timing, dependency risk, runway needed to resolve an open
  question (access, a skill still developing, etc.).
- **Honesty bar:** what makes this project's evidence real rather than a
  demo — a real eval harness, a real before/after number, a documented real
  failure, a real external user. Say what it is for this specific project,
  not the generic tier-3 definition.

